{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/81954-wraith-the-oblivion-marriage-in-wraith-my-two-oboli","id":81954,"title":"[Wraith: The Oblivion] Marriage in Wraith (my two oboli)","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Wraith: The Oblivion"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-02-28T22:34:53-08:00","id":81954,"title":"[Wraith: The Oblivion] Marriage in Wraith (my two oboli)","content":"<span style=\"font-family:courier new\">A few folks asked Deadguy a question about this. Rather than take up more space in that thread, I figured I\u2019d take a stab in a new thread. <br /> </span> <div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-family:courier new\">Marriage in Wraith</span></div> <span style=\"font-family:courier new\"> <br /> I think the institution of marriage has narrow applications in Stygian society. The only occurrence of marriage would be pre-existing relationships. For example, IIRC, John Soulis and his wife are still married (Oh, and what a happy, fairy tale marriage they had). Since Wraiths are souls that have things holding them back, I could see people who identify themselves as couples continue that on the other side of the Shroud. As in life, so on in death. Kind of like the Maitlands in <i>Beetlejuice</i>.<br /> <br /> Being one of the Restless Dead means there\u2019s something keeping you around. Finding that special ghostly someone, settling down, and having 2.5 striplings and a barghest in a haunt with a white picket-fence isn\u2019t really the act of someone who can\u2019t/won\u2019t \u201cmove on.\u201d Not that love and romance don\u2019t have any place in the Underworld; quite the contrary. Those are powerful emotions (and Passions). Just ask Erik. Thematically, it just feels out-of-place with the Wraith setting. Unless you have a terribly bleak view on marriage, I would avoid incorporating it into your games. <br /> <br /> [On the other hand, I think marriage would be fascinating to incorporate into a Changeling game. Imagine: Redcap wedding!]<br /> <br /> My two oboli. Agree? Disagree? </span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:8px\"><span style=\"font-family:comic sans ms\">&lt;signature&gt; something witty &lt;/signature&gt;</span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/302-nazfool","name":"Nazfool","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar302_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"753"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-01T00:27:53-08:00","id":81985,"title":"","content":"I agree. Especially with the Smiling Lord in charge (if that's how you play), I can't see Stygia taking a stance on the idea of marriage one way or the other. Maybe some Far Shores offer it as a &quot;perk,&quot; or the Jade Empire uses it to forge alliances, but the people in charge of Stygia don't give a toss.<br /> <br /> I can see the Legion of Fate doing it though, as well as the Laughing Legion. But not a decree from the Onyx Tower, no.<br /> <br /> Also, a Redcap wedding would be just like the Red Wedding, but with more eating the bodies afterwords.<br /> <br /> Cheers!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If you don't use an Oxford comma, I feel bad for you, son,<br /> 'Cuz I got ninety-nine problems, but clarity ain't one.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/408-nothing","name":"nothing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar408_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"572"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-01T09:08:23-08:00","id":82124,"title":"","content":"Actually I would argue the opposite. First off WtO itself does not have to be a bleak, depressing game, it gets stereotyped as a bleak, depressing game. Yes it wears a grimdark cloak but if you dig beyond that you can see the aspects of hope and spirituality. And it's the only CWoD with an achievable end, where you can actually win. With VtM you're damned and no matter how powerful you become there will always be someone older and more powerful than you. WtA, you're fighting a losing war because of the mistakes of your ancestors and if you're lucky you might die saving the world for another night. MtAs, Big Brother has won and you're in many ways a terrorist whose ideas for the world probably aren't much better than Big Brother's. With WtO, yes you are dead but if you chose you can address what's holding you back, the mistakes and shortcomings of your life, gaining closure before ultimately passing on as you were meant to.<br /> <br /> Marriage and love can still be very important to a wraith as Erik's story has shown. And yes, it can be a positive thing and work in WtO, more so than in some of the other games. In fact they have many potential avenues to explore said concepts. What happens when a couple die together in an accident and become wraiths? Are they still together after death? How do their Shadows react to this? Do they work to claim them both or drive them apart? What if they're reaped by two different groups, say by the Hierarchy and the Renegades respectively, and don't meet each again until months later, finding themselves on opposing sides? Do they strive together to achieve Transcendence or to simply survive in the Underworld? For that matter what would Transcendence mean to them? Would they both even agree to it?<br /> <br /> And those are just things I pulled out without even thinking too hard on it.<br /> <br /> Of course like with anything, love and marriage can be twisted into something dark and terrible. A great example of this can be seen in the Vampire: the Masquerade Bloodlines game. One of the early quests sees you enter a hotel haunted by a pair of ghosts, a husband and wife who went on vacation there with their kids years ago. While there the husband became convinced that his wife was having an affair which she wasn't and snapped. He killed his wife and children, set fire to the hotel and killed himself. However it didn't end there and the two of them became wraiths. The husband, obsessed with keeping his wife has trapped her in the hotel while trying to keep everyone else out.<br /> <br /> Ultimately it's all about how the players and the ST handle it. Even the best ideas can fall apart horribly if handled poorly.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_yBS2EZFduI?t=2m13s\" target=\"_blank\">Homo sapiens. What an inventive, invincible species. It's only a few million years since they crawled up out of the mud and learned to walk. Puny, defenceless bipeds. They've survived flood, famine and plague. They've survived cosmic wars and holocausts. And now, here they are, out among the stars, waiting to begin a new life. Ready to outsit eternity. They're indomitable. Indomitable.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/420-akatsukileader13","name":"AkatsukiLeader13","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar420_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1637"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-01T09:31:18-08:00","id":82131,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:courier new\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AkatsukiLeader13</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/81954-wraith-the-oblivion-marriage-in-wraith-my-two-oboli?p=82124#post82124\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually I would argue the opposite.<br /> Marriage and love can still be very important to a wraith as Erik's story has shown. What happens when a couple die together in an accident and become wraiths? Are they still together after death? How do their Shadows react to this? Do they work to claim them both or drive them apart? What if they're reaped by two different groups, say by the Hierarchy and the Renegades respectively, and don't meet each again until months later, finding themselves on opposing sides? Do they strive together to achieve Transcendence or to simply survive in the Underworld? For that matter what would Transcendence mean to them? Would they both even agree to it?</div> </div> </div> </div>With respect, I don\u2019t think you actually disagreed with anything I wrote. In fact, I think you confirmed it:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The only occurrence of marriage would be pre-existing relationships. \u2026 I could see people who identify themselves as couples continue that on the other side of the Shroud.<br /> <br /> Not that love and romance don\u2019t have any place in the Underworld; quite the contrary. Those are powerful emotions (and Passions). Just ask Erik. </div> </div> </div>I think symbolic marriages might be utilized in some circles. I could easily imagine the Legion of Fate using for its iconic symbolism. I could also see the Laughing Legion using it with a cross between symbolism and parody. As Nothing mentioned, Heretics and the Jade Empire might use for those reasons. In a weird way, I could also see Nephwracks (maybe even some Onceborn) using perversions of wedding ceremonies to cement alliances.<br /> <br /> The difference is a thematic one. As a symbolic ritual, yes, I could see Wraiths utilizing it. In these cases, it wouldn\u2019t be just a matter of \u201cgetting married.\u201d It\u2019s the symbolism and intent behind the ceremony, whether ritualized alliance-forming (which many cultures do, to my understanding), parody, or Shadow-eaten perversion. A drive-thru Las Vegas Elvis minister saying, \u201cYou may kiss the bride,\u201d invokes much different imagery than a Barker-esque Nephwrack saying the same thing.<br /> <br /> Would Anne and Andy run off to their Legion\u2019s Chaplin and get hitched? It just seems inappropriate to the setting.<br /> Would an Anacreon marry a Dybbuk to reinforce a working relationship between the Kingdom of Iron and the Kingdom of Wire? Maybe\u2026<br /> <br /> I think that a juxtaposition of such incongruous elements \u2013 \u201cgetting married\u201d and being dead \u2013 might invoke an inappropriate comedic tone. Again, this is my take. If a troupe wants more Lydia and less Kirsty, there\u2019s nothing wrong with that. </span><br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:8px\"><span style=\"font-family:comic sans ms\">&lt;signature&gt; something witty &lt;/signature&gt;</span></span>","edited":{"userid":302,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/302-nazfool","user":"Nazfool","date":". 03-01-2014, 12:40 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/302-nazfool","name":"Nazfool","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar302_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"753"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-01T14:48:46-08:00","id":82322,"title":"","content":"Tragically, it does seem that there is an unfortunate focus on Wraith being such an unrelentingly \u201cbleak and depressing\u201d game, as AkatsukiLeader13, so adroitly put it and it definitely doesn\u2019t have to be. It can be a world of hope for many.<br /> <br /> I also agree with Akatsuki's statement: \u201cmarriage and love can be very important to a wraith.\u201d <br /> <br /> First marriage doesn\u2019t anthropologically speaking originate in a top down order, so I would think it hardly a matter to an individual if the \u201cgovernment\u201d or other leadership have necessarily instituted a formal version of it. For a wraith, I would suggest, it would stem from their own personal beliefs while they had been alive and the importance to them in their society, culture, spiritual background and personal belief from which they originated.<br /> <br /> While there can be \u201ctwisted\u201d views in the realm of the undead as there are \u201ctwisted\u201d views of many things in the world of the living, there would also be pure and true views as well.<br /> <br /> If there were no hope or \u201clife\u201d to live in the underworld pretty much everyone would be doing nothing but mope around and quickly succumb to Oblivion.<br /> <br /> Charon, as any good emperor, set up a system of merit and reward and gave people in society something to strive for, a purpose and a place while being in the underworld. He also promoted beautifying the Underworld with art and culture.<br /> <br /> Why, for example, would Stygia have the Library of Alexandria, if one could not have other interests than resolving things holding you back from moving on? Then again, some might not want to resolve them, anymore than they did while alive. Yet again, a wraith might have not been able to do certain things in life, whether from age, fear, opportunity, illness, and so forth, and may feel more freedom to explore things here, even beautiful things here.<br /> <br /> As it is, I think that \u201cmarriage\u201d in Wraith would fit very much into the world, since it could be that people find love on the other side of the Shroud and some would want that sense of commitment and belonging reflected from their personal beliefs and culture while the had been in the world of the Quick. <br /> <br /> In the Jade Kingdom too you have \u201cfamilies,\u201d so this would also make sense. I believe I mentioned before a wonderful ghost book, called \u201cThe Ghost Bride\u201d based off an ancient Chinese practice:<br /> <br /> \u201cIn Chinese tradition, a <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghost_marriage_%28Chinese%29\" target=\"_blank\">ghost marriage</a> (also known as a <i>Minghun</i> or <i>spirit marriage</i>) is a marriage in which one or both parties are deceased.<sup><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghosts_in_Chinese_culture#cite_note-25\" target=\"_blank\">[25]</a></sup> A ghost marriage was usually set up by the family of the deceased and performed for a number of reasons, including the marriage of a couple previously engaged before one member\u2019s death, to integrate an unmarried daughter into a patrilineage, to ensure the family line is continued, or to maintain that no younger brother is married before an elder brother.<sup><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghosts_in_Chinese_culture#cite_note-26\" target=\"_blank\">[26]</a>\u201d -http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghosts_in_Chinese_culture</sup><br /> <br /> I would think in the Iron Kingdom \u201cgetting married\u201d, wraith to wraith, would also very much make sense for more Western and for simple \u201chuman\u201d reasons.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1343-khaleesi","name":"Khaleesi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1343_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"39"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-01T16:28:03-08:00","id":82389,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:courier new\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Khaleesi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/81954-wraith-the-oblivion-marriage-in-wraith-my-two-oboli?p=82322#post82322\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Tragically, it does seem that there is an unfortunate focus on Wraith being such an unrelentingly \u201cbleak and depressing\u201d game, as AkatsukiLeader13, so adroitly put it and it definitely doesn\u2019t have to be. It can be a world of hope for many.<br /> <br /> I also agree with Akatsuki's statement: \u201cmarriage and love can be very important to a wraith.\u201d<br /> <br /> First marriage doesn\u2019t anthropologically speaking originate in a top down order, so I would think it hardly a matter to an individual if the \u201cgovernment\u201d or other leadership have necessarily instituted a formal version of it. For a wraith, I would suggest, it would stem from their own personal beliefs while they had been alive and the importance to them in their society, culture, spiritual background and personal belief from which they originated.<br /> <br /> While there can be \u201ctwisted\u201d views in the realm of the undead as there are \u201ctwisted\u201d views of many things in the world of the living, there would also be pure and true views as well.<br /> <br /> If there were no hope or \u201clife\u201d to live in the underworld pretty much everyone would be doing nothing but mope around and quickly succumb to Oblivion.<br /> <br /> Charon, as any good emperor, set up a system of merit and reward and gave people in society something to strive for, a purpose and a place while being in the underworld. He also promoted beautifying the Underworld with art and culture.<br /> <br /> Why, for example, would Stygia have the Library of Alexandria, if one could not have other interests than resolving things holding you back from moving on? Then again, some might not want to resolve them, anymore than they did while alive. Yet again, a wraith might have not been able to do certain things in life, whether from age, fear, opportunity, illness, and so forth, and may feel more freedom to explore things here, even beautiful things here.<br /> <br /> As it is, I think that \u201cmarriage\u201d in Wraith would fit very much into the world, since it could be that people find love on the other side of the Shroud and some would want that sense of commitment and belonging reflected from their personal beliefs and culture while the had been in the world of the Quick.<br /> <br /> In the Jade Kingdom too you have \u201cfamilies,\u201d so this would also make sense. I believe I mentioned before a wonderful ghost book, called \u201cThe Ghost Bride\u201d based off an ancient Chinese practice:<br /> <br /> \u201cIn Chinese tradition, a <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghost_marriage_%28Chinese%29\" target=\"_blank\">ghost marriage</a> (also known as a <i>Minghun</i> or <i>spirit marriage</i>) is a marriage in which one or both parties are deceased.<sup><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghosts_in_Chinese_culture#cite_note-25\" target=\"_blank\">[25]</a></sup> A ghost marriage was usually set up by the family of the deceased and performed for a number of reasons, including the marriage of a couple previously engaged before one member\u2019s death, to integrate an unmarried daughter into a patrilineage, to ensure the family line is continued, or to maintain that no younger brother is married before an elder brother.<sup><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghosts_in_Chinese_culture#cite_note-26\" target=\"_blank\">[26]</a>\u201d -http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghosts_in_Chinese_culture</sup><br /> <br /> I would think in the Iron Kingdom \u201cgetting married\u201d, wraith to wraith, would also very much make sense for more Western and for simple \u201chuman\u201d reasons.<br /> </div> </div> </div> </div> <br /> Kewl. <br /> <br /> Sounds like you have it doped out. <br /> <br /> To clarify, I\u2019m not defending my position; I\u2019m just explaining my perspective. <br /> <br /> Also, I never mentioned Wraith being bleak and depressing. What did I say that gives that impression?<br /> </span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:8px\"><span style=\"font-family:comic sans ms\">&lt;signature&gt; something witty &lt;/signature&gt;</span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/302-nazfool","name":"Nazfool","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar302_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"753"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-02T11:20:32-08:00","id":82708,"title":"","content":"Dear Nazfool,<br /> <br /> I know you must really like Wraith too, as you posted such a nice reply on my first thread here on &quot;Why I Love Wraith.&quot; I really enjoy seeing different views as it gives new insights or lets us think about things. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Also, posts, like texts messages or emails, can leave out fine nuances or explanations so we can often miss the shades of meaning. Maybe, I feel that your expression of &quot;getting married&quot; being thematically unfitting in the underworld is a &quot;bleak and depressing&quot; one, because I feel it would or rather could be something beautiful and hopeful and fits in wonderfully with the theme. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> An example, lets say could be someone who always wanted to get married and never did, so to find someone they love and to &quot;get married&quot; may be one of their passions or a couple in route to their wedding died tragically and were reunited on the other side who may want to fulfill that lost desire or perhaps someone's betrothed never came back from some war and they were united in the underworld. I absolutely agree with your words that: &quot;<span style=\"font-family:courier new\">Not that love and romance don\u2019t have any place in the Underworld; quite the contrary.&quot;, </span>but for some, lets say someone with old fashioned ideas of what would look like, it could only be fulfilled in the context of marriage, particularly depending on the society they came from and their personal codes, morals, hopes, dreams, passions, etc.<br /> <br /> Many happy wishes!<br /> <br /> Khaleesi","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1343-khaleesi","name":"Khaleesi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1343_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"39"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-02T21:36:03-08:00","id":82954,"title":"","content":"Personally, I'm anti-marriage, because I don't believe that love requires any legitimacy from some third-party &quot;authority.&quot; That being said, I'm sure that marriage occurs between wraiths, but if there was ever a Stygian &quot;institution&quot; for it, I feel that it must have fallen by the wayside after Charon's disappearance.<br /> <br /> Cheers!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If you don't use an Oxford comma, I feel bad for you, son,<br /> 'Cuz I got ninety-nine problems, but clarity ain't one.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/408-nothing","name":"nothing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar408_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"572"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-08T02:23:21-08:00","id":86608,"title":"","content":"is the &quot;Why I love Wraith&quot; thread still around? Could not find it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;No live organism can continue for long to exist sanely under conditions of absolute reality; even larks and katydids are supposed, by some, to dream.&quot; - Shirley Jackson","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1227-shenron","name":"Shenron","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1227_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"193"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-08T06:35:09-08:00","id":86716,"title":"","content":"I think it would make for an interesting story hook but all in all would seem pretty irrelevant in Wraith society.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2467-iris","name":"Iris","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2467_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-10T21:33:59-07:00","id":88637,"title":"","content":"@Shenron - The &quot;Why I Love Wraith&quot; post is here: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/26695-why-i-love-wraith-the-oblivion\">http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/m...h-the-oblivion</a>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1343-khaleesi","name":"Khaleesi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1343_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"39"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-10T21:59:30-07:00","id":88660,"title":"","content":"I absolutely agree with Khaleesi's post! I think it would be very important in wraith society stemming from the social and cultural anthropological backgrounds that everyone would come from. And as Iris says, it would provide for some interesting story hooks. Like things in the real world, not everyone has to be into the social norms, customs and mores of a society and are free to make other choices. Hence their are &quot;Renegades&quot; and &quot;renegades&quot;. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2386-katrina","name":"Katrina","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2386_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"25"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}